<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px; margin-bottom: 20px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Once upon a Time... – Lesson # 2</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Legends of Panama</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> Once upon a Time...</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of the main plot and characters in short stories. Reading: Extract key facts and details from informational texts. Phonemic Awareness: Demonstrate knowledge of phonemic awareness by reading base words and inflectional endings (-s, -es, -ed, -ing).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show comprehension of the main plot and characters of simple legends (e.g., "The hero was fighting the monster when the village was attacked."). Can extract key facts from texts about Panamanian legends (e.g., "The legend of La India Dormida takes place in El Valle de Antón."). Can read words with past tense (-ed) /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ and recognize their pronunciation (e.g., emphasis goes in the pronunciation of /ɪd/ in "waited" in the sentence, "The villagers waited for the hero.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 30px;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 35%; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 10px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 15%; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and asks them to quickly recall 3-4 vocabulary words they learned in the previous lesson related to "legends," such as `hero`, `monster`, `village`, or `story`. The teacher shows flashcards of these words or related images and asks, "What do you see?" or "What is this word?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces the idea of reading a short Panamanian legend. "Today, we are going to read a story from Panama. It's a `legend`! Does anyone remember what a `legend` is?" The teacher holds up a picture of a typical Panamanian village or a natural landscape like a `river` or `mountain`, saying, "This `legend` takes place in a beautiful `village` near a `river`." The teacher clearly pronounces key vocabulary like `legend`, `village`, `hero`, `monster`, `treasure`. The teacher models reading a short sentence from the upcoming text, like, "Once upon a time, a brave `hero` lived in a small `village`." focusing on natural intonation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a `legend` a true story or an old story that people tell?" (Expected answer: An old story.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Do `heroes` usually fight `monsters` or help people?" (Expected answer: Help people / Fight monsters.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A simplified narrative excerpt of a well-known Panamanian legend (e.g., "La Tulivieja" or "El Chivo") adapted for A2.2 level, approximately 80-100 words. The text is printed clearly, possibly with a relevant illustration. It includes target grammar (Past Simple, Past Continuous) and vocabulary (e.g., `once upon a time`, `village`, `monster`, `ghost`, `river`, `appeared`, `was searching`).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> The student can understand the main points of short, simple texts on familiar matters, extracting predictable information from simple everyday material.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading for Gist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pre-reading:</b> Teacher displays the title of the legend and a picture. "Look at the title and the picture. What do you think this `legend` is about? A `hero`? A `monster`?" Students share ideas in pairs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Reading (Gist):</b> Students read the `legend` individually for the first time. Teacher asks a general question: "What is the main problem in the `story`?" or "Who is the main `creature` in this `legend`?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Reading (True/False):</b> Students read the `legend` again. Teacher provides 3-4 True/False statements about the main plot and characters. E.g., "The `ghost` lived in the `town`." (False) "The `villagers` were `scared` of the `monster`." (True). Students mark their answers.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Reading.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading comprehension of main plot and characters; extraction of key facts; recognition of Past Simple and Past Continuous verbs; pronunciation of -ed endings (/t/, /d/, /ɪd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice (Gap-fill):</b> Teacher distributes a handout with sentences from the `legend` containing blanks. Students fill in the blanks using a word bank of target vocabulary (e.g., `hero`, `monster`, `village`, `river`, `appeared`, `fought`, `was hiding`). Example: "The `brave` _____ `fought` the evil _____." (hero, monster) or "The `creature` _____ in the `river` `at night`." (appeared/was hiding).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-controlled (Reading for Details & Sequencing):</b> Students answer specific comprehension questions about the text that require extracting key facts and details, focusing on past events and sequence. Examples: "Where did the `legend` take place?" "What was the `hero` doing when the `monster` `appeared`?" (using past continuous). "First, what happened? Then, what happened?" (using `first`, `then`).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Peer Check & Pronunciation Focus:</b> In pairs, students read aloud selected sentences from the `legend` that contain verbs with past tense -ed endings (e.g., `waited`, `walked`, `wanted`, `celebrated`, `rescued`, `attacked`). The teacher circulates, listens, and models the correct pronunciation of /t/, /d/, or /ɪd/ for these words. For example, "The villagers `waited` for the `hero`." (emphasizing /ɪd/ in `waited`). "The `hero` `rescued` the `village`." (emphasizing /d/ in `rescued`). "The `witch` `attacked` the `town`." (emphasizing /t/ in `attacked`). Students identify which sound they hear.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-17 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Legends of Panama".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative summarization and event sequencing.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Activity: "Legend Timeline"</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (3-4 students). Each group receives strips of paper with jumbled events from the `legend` they just read.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Their task is to read the strips and put them in the correct chronological order, creating a simple timeline of the `story`.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">After arranging the events, each group takes turns telling the `story` using the sequenced strips. They are encouraged to use time markers and sequencing words like `once upon a time`, `first`, `then`, `after that`, `suddenly`, `in the end`. They also practice using past simple and past continuous (e.g., "The `hero` `was walking` `when` the `monster` `appeared`.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher observes groups, providing support and noting fluency in reading and sequencing.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation during group work and a short individual comprehension check.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students complete a short individual task where they identify the main characters and the main plot of the legend. This could be a "Who did what?" matching activity or filling in a graphic organizer (e.g., "Character: _____, Role: _____ / Main Event 1: _____, Main Event 2: _____"). They also answer one question focused on extracting a key fact. E.g., "What was the `monster`'s special power?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy in identifying main plot points.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct identification of characters and their roles.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to extract key facts and details from the text.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Optional: Teacher notes on pronunciation of -ed endings during group reading, if time permits for individual checks.)</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer on a slip of paper:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one new thing you learned about reading `legends` today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to understand the `story`? Why?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What part of the `legend` was most interesting to read?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What reading strategy helped you the most today (e.g., looking at pictures, looking for key words, re-reading)?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What reading comprehension strategies worked well for the students? Did they successfully extract key facts and understand the main plot and characters?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How effective was the pronunciation practice for -ed endings? Do students need more focused practice on `/t/`, `/d/`, or `/ɪd/` sounds?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or scaffolds are needed for the next lesson (Speaking & Interaction) to ensure students can talk about `legends` using the target vocabulary and grammar confidently? I will ensure the speaking activities in Lesson 3 allow students to retell parts of this legend or similar ones.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> In the next lesson (Lesson 3: Speaking & Interaction), students will use the vocabulary and grammar from this lesson to discuss and orally narrate parts of `legends` with their peers, focusing on telling a `story` and sequencing events.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>